<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kagyu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kagyu school</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kagyu is one of the main schools of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, tracing its teaching lineage from the Indian masters Tilopa and Naropa through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> Marpa, Milarepa and Gampopa in the 11th and 12th centuries. Gampopa's disciples founded several sub-schools, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drikung-kagyu">Drikung Kagyu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drukpa-kagyu">Drukpa Kagyu</a> and Phagdru Kagyu, the last linked to the Phagmodrupa rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kagyu.jpg" alt="Kagyu" width="320" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahasiddha_Naropa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
